"""
# In-development tag — mark a spec as work-in-progress and EXPECTED TO FAIL

@tag:stdlib
@tag:testing

## What this is

A spec file whose header carries the comment

    # @tag:in-development

is declared WORK IN PROGRESS. It is expected to fail. It is not a passing
test, and it is emphatically not a silent skip.

## Why the tag spelling is `@tag:in-development`

It reuses the `@tag:<name>` source-comment convention that is already the
only tag channel in this tree (censused 2026-08-23: 57 distinct names,
1022 occurrences — `api`, `stdlib`, `system`, `gpu`, ...). Multi-word tag
names are ALREADY hyphenated in that census (`back-compat`,
`api-individual`, `api-grouped`, `evidence-source-contract`,
`linux-hosted-wm`), so `in-development` is the conventional spelling and
`in_development` would be the deviation.

Deliberately NOT reused, and why:

  - `pending` — `std.spec.pending()` already means "this example is a
    placeholder that ran nothing". The runner counts it on a SEPARATE
    channel from skips (`count_real_skips` in test_runner_single.spl) and
    the zero-executed greenwash guard exists precisely to stop a file of
    pending() reading as green. In-development is the opposite shape: the
    test is WRITTEN and RUNS, it just does not pass yet.
  - `skip` — `skip()`/`skip_it()` mean "this environment cannot run it"
    (no GPU, wrong OS). That is a capability statement about the HOST.
    In-development is a statement about the CODE UNDER TEST.
  - `wip` — no occurrences in the tree; inventing an abbreviation when the
    census shows the convention is spelled-out lowercase words would be a
    parallel scheme.

## Semantics (decided explicitly — see the design record)

| invocation | tagged spec FAILS | tagged spec PASSES |
|---|---|---|
| whole-suite (`simple test`) | NOT a suite failure. Counted and printed as an in-development skip. | Reported loudly as an UNEXPECTED PASS: it is ready to be promoted. |
| explicit path (`simple test path/to/x_spec.spl`) | runs and FAILS honestly, normal red, normal exit code | runs and passes, plus an advisory that the tag can now come off |

Two consequences are deliberate and are the whole point:

1. **"Skip" here means skipped from the FAILURE VERDICT, not skipped from
   EXECUTION.** The file is still run. A file that is never executed can
   never be observed to have started passing, so a not-run design makes
   requirement (4) — report the unexpected pass — unimplementable, and
   in-development tests would rot forever. Running-and-neutralising costs
   the suite the test's runtime and buys back the promotion signal.
2. **An in-development skip is never invisible.** It is printed per file
   and totalled in the summary. An invisible skip is how debt disappears;
   this module exists to make the debt countable.

## API for other lanes

This module is PURE (text in, verdict out) and declares no externs, so it
adds no direct `rt_*` call site to the ratchet. Callers that have a path
rather than a source do their own read and hand the text in.

Statistics / tag-listing tools should call `spec_tags(source)` and
`source_is_in_development(source)` rather than re-grepping for `@tag:`,
and should recognise the emitted lines by the `IN_DEVELOPMENT_*_MARKER`
constants rather than by matching prose.

    use std.spec.in_development.{
        IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG, spec_tags, source_has_tag,
        source_is_in_development, in_development_explicitly_targeted,
        InDevelopmentOutcome, classify_in_development,
        IN_DEVELOPMENT_SKIP_MARKER, IN_DEVELOPMENT_UNEXPECTED_PASS_MARKER,
        in_development_skip_line, in_development_unexpected_pass_line,
        in_development_explicit_line, in_development_summary_line
    }
"""

# The canonical tag name. Every producer and consumer spells it via this
# constant so the string exists once.
val IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG: text = "in-development"

# The annotation prefix shared with every other `@tag:` in the tree.
val TAG_PREFIX: text = "@tag:"

# Line markers. Machine-readable, stable, and grep-anchorable: the sibling
# statistics lane keys off these, NOT off the surrounding prose.
val IN_DEVELOPMENT_SKIP_MARKER: text = "IN-DEVELOPMENT SKIP"
val IN_DEVELOPMENT_UNEXPECTED_PASS_MARKER: text = "IN-DEVELOPMENT UNEXPECTED PASS"
val IN_DEVELOPMENT_EXPLICIT_MARKER: text = "IN-DEVELOPMENT EXPLICIT"
val IN_DEVELOPMENT_SUMMARY_MARKER: text = "In-development:"

# ================================================================
# TAG EXTRACTION
# ================================================================

fn _is_tag_char(c: i64) -> bool:
    """Characters that may appear in a tag name: [A-Za-z0-9_-]."""
    if c >= 97 and c <= 122:
        return true
    if c >= 65 and c <= 90:
        return true
    if c >= 48 and c <= 57:
        return true
    if c == 95:
        return true
    if c == 45:
        return true
    false

fn spec_tags(source: text) -> [text]:
    """Extract every `@tag:<name>` occurring in `source`, in order, with
    duplicates removed.

    This is the CANONICAL extractor. Tools that want the tag set of a spec
    call this rather than re-implementing a grep, so that a change to the
    tag grammar lands in exactly one place.

    Scans raw text on purpose: `@tag:` appears both in `#` line comments
    and inside `\"\"\"` docstring headers (see engine_probe.spl), and both
    are legitimate tag sites in this tree.
    """
    var out: [text] = []
    # contains_key + bracket assign only: Dict.len()/.get() are unreliable
    # under native codegen (doc/07_guide/language/dict_native_pitfalls.md).
    var seen: dict<text, bool> = {}
    val n = source.len()
    val plen = TAG_PREFIX.len()
    var i = 0
    while i + plen <= n:
        if source.char_code_at(i) == 64 and source.slice(i, i + plen) == TAG_PREFIX:
            var j = i + plen
            while j < n and _is_tag_char(source.char_code_at(j)):
                j = j + 1
            if j > i + plen:
                val name = source.slice(i + plen, j)
                if not seen.contains_key(name):
                    seen[name] = true
                    out.push(name)
            i = j
        else:
            i = i + 1
    out

fn source_has_tag(source: text, tag: text) -> bool:
    """True when `source` carries `@tag:<tag>`.

    Exact-name match, not a substring match: `@tag:in-development-notes`
    must NOT satisfy a query for `in-development`.
    """
    for t in spec_tags(source):
        if t == tag:
            return true
    false

fn source_is_in_development(source: text) -> bool:
    """True when a spec's SOURCE declares `# @tag:in-development`."""
    source_has_tag(source, IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG)

# ================================================================
# EXPLICIT-PATH TARGETING
# ================================================================

fn _basename_matches(file_path: text, requested: text) -> bool:
    if file_path == requested:
        return true
    # `test/x_spec.spl` requested as `./test/x_spec.spl`, or the file given
    # as an absolute path while the request was repo-relative (or vice
    # versa). Suffix match on a FULL path component only — never a bare
    # substring, which would make `a/b_spec.spl` match `b_spec.spl` inside
    # an unrelated directory only by accident of naming.
    if file_path.ends_with("/" + requested):
        return true
    if requested.ends_with("/" + file_path):
        return true
    false

fn in_development_explicitly_targeted(
    file_path: text,
    path_explicit: bool,
    requested_paths: [text]
) -> bool:
    """True when this exact spec FILE was named on the command line.

    Reuses `TestOptions.path_explicit` + `TestOptions.paths`, which already
    exist — no new option field, and nothing in a sibling lane's struct had
    to change.

    A DIRECTORY target does not count as explicit for the files beneath it:
    `simple test test/01_unit` is a sweep, and a sweep must get the
    whole-suite (neutralised) semantics. Only naming the file itself opts
    into the honest red.
    """
    if not path_explicit:
        return false
    for r in requested_paths:
        if r != "" and _basename_matches(file_path, r):
            return true
    false

# ================================================================
# OUTCOME CLASSIFICATION
# ================================================================

enum InDevelopmentOutcome:
    NotInDevelopment      # no tag — the runner's ordinary rules apply
    ExpectedFailure       # tagged and failed: neutralised, counted, printed
    UnexpectedPass        # tagged and passed: reported loudly, ready to promote
    ExplicitRun           # tagged but named on the command line: honest verdict

fn classify_in_development(
    is_tagged: bool,
    explicitly_targeted: bool,
    failed: i64,
    passed: i64,
    errored: bool
) -> InDevelopmentOutcome:
    """The single classifier. Both the per-file and the summary surfaces
    route through this rather than re-deriving the rule, for the same
    reason `test_file_result_outcome_class` exists in the runner types:
    two spellings of one rule is how the two surfaces come to disagree.

    `errored` folds in `timed_out` and a non-empty `error`: a WIP spec that
    crashes or times out is still just failing, and must not escape through
    the error channel into a suite-level red.
    """
    if not is_tagged:
        return InDevelopmentOutcome.NotInDevelopment
    if explicitly_targeted:
        return InDevelopmentOutcome.ExplicitRun
    if failed > 0 or errored:
        return InDevelopmentOutcome.ExpectedFailure
    if passed > 0:
        return InDevelopmentOutcome.UnexpectedPass
    # Tagged, nothing failed, nothing passed: the file executed no example
    # at all. That is NOT a promotion signal — treating it as one would let
    # an empty WIP file announce itself as ready. It is the expected
    # not-working-yet state.
    InDevelopmentOutcome.ExpectedFailure

# ================================================================
# REPORT LINES
# ================================================================

fn in_development_skip_line(path: text, expected_failures: i64) -> text:
    """Per-file line for a tagged spec that failed as expected."""
    "{IN_DEVELOPMENT_SKIP_MARKER} {path} ({expected_failures} expected failure(s); @tag:{IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG})"

fn in_development_unexpected_pass_line(path: text, passed: i64) -> text:
    """Per-file line for a tagged spec that PASSED.

    Loud on purpose. This is good news that must not be silent: the test is
    ready to be promoted, and the action is concrete (delete the tag), so
    the line says so rather than leaving the reader to infer it.
    """
    "{IN_DEVELOPMENT_UNEXPECTED_PASS_MARKER} {path} ({passed} example(s) passed) — ready to promote: remove `# @tag:{IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG}`"

fn in_development_explicit_line(path: text) -> text:
    """Advisory printed when a tagged spec is run by explicit path.

    It runs and reports honestly; this line only explains why a red here is
    not the same red the suite would have shown.
    """
    "{IN_DEVELOPMENT_EXPLICIT_MARKER} {path} — `@tag:{IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG}`; explicit-path run reports the honest verdict (the suite would neutralise it)"

fn in_development_summary_line(skipped: i64, unexpected_passes: i64) -> text:
    """The summary row. Printed whenever EITHER count is non-zero.

    Never hidden behind a verbose flag: an in-development test that nobody
    can see the count of is exactly the invisible debt this feature exists
    to prevent.
    """
    if unexpected_passes > 0:
        return "{IN_DEVELOPMENT_SUMMARY_MARKER} {skipped} skipped (expected to fail), {unexpected_passes} UNEXPECTED PASS (ready to promote)"
    "{IN_DEVELOPMENT_SUMMARY_MARKER} {skipped} skipped (expected to fail)"

export IN_DEVELOPMENT_TAG, TAG_PREFIX
export IN_DEVELOPMENT_SKIP_MARKER, IN_DEVELOPMENT_UNEXPECTED_PASS_MARKER
export IN_DEVELOPMENT_EXPLICIT_MARKER, IN_DEVELOPMENT_SUMMARY_MARKER
export spec_tags, source_has_tag, source_is_in_development
export in_development_explicitly_targeted
export InDevelopmentOutcome, classify_in_development
export in_development_skip_line, in_development_unexpected_pass_line
export in_development_explicit_line, in_development_summary_line
